WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.820
It’s Professor Dave; let’s introduce the
distributive property.

00:00:10.580 --> 00:00:15.460
As we learn math, we will see that there are
certain properties of numbers that are very

00:00:15.470 --> 00:00:16.730
important.

00:00:16.730 --> 00:00:22.520
We already learned about the commutative property
and the associative property, and we saw how

00:00:22.520 --> 00:00:28.510
addition and multiplication abide by these
properties, while subtraction and division

00:00:28.510 --> 00:00:29.580
do not.

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:33.730
Now let’s learn another property, the distributive
property.

00:00:33.730 --> 00:00:37.690
To see how this works, let’s look at some
more apples.

00:00:37.690 --> 00:00:41.379
Say we have five piles of seven apples.

00:00:41.379 --> 00:00:46.760
Five piles times seven apples per pile gives
us thirty-five apples.

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:51.299
What if we split each pile up into a group
of three and a group of four?

00:00:51.299 --> 00:00:53.890
There are a few ways we could represent this.

00:00:53.890 --> 00:00:59.159
Let’s take the seven and split it up into
three plus four, in parentheses.

00:00:59.159 --> 00:01:04.510
This is clearly the same value we once had,
because three plus four is seven, which makes

00:01:04.510 --> 00:01:10.440
sense because rearranging the apples shouldn’t
change the number of them that are present.

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:16.780
But we could also say that we have five piles
of three and five piles of four.

00:01:16.780 --> 00:01:20.430
That means five times three plus five times
four.

00:01:20.430 --> 00:01:26.360
Visually, we are just describing what we see,
but mathematically, we have just derived the

00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:28.420
distributive property.

00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:34.110
When we have a number that is multiplied by
a sum or difference, we can distribute that

00:01:34.110 --> 00:01:38.580
number across each value within the sum or
difference.

00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:43.550
In this case, that means five times three
plus five times four.

00:01:43.550 --> 00:01:49.270
That gives us fifteen plus twenty, which is
indeed still thirty-five.

00:01:49.270 --> 00:01:54.350
So whether we add the numbers in the parentheses
first and then multiply by the other number,

00:01:54.350 --> 00:02:00.490
or distribute the number across the sum, we
will get the same result, and that is the

00:02:00.490 --> 00:02:06.740
essence of the distributive property, which
will be very important once we get to algebra.

00:02:06.740 --> 00:02:09.299
We can even use this property in reverse.

00:02:09.299 --> 00:02:12.799
Let’s say we have five times seventeen.

00:02:12.799 --> 00:02:19.019
If we don’t want to write down a calculation,
we can easily do this in our heads if we split

00:02:19.019 --> 00:02:22.549
seventeen up into ten plus five plus two.

00:02:22.549 --> 00:02:29.230
Now, we can distribute the five across the
sum, and rewrite this as five times ten plus

00:02:29.230 --> 00:02:32.909
five times five plus five times two.

00:02:32.909 --> 00:02:38.450
These sums can be done easily in your head,
and we get fifty plus twenty-five plus ten,

00:02:38.450 --> 00:02:40.439
and that’s eighty-five.

00:02:40.439 --> 00:02:45.249
This sort of mental trickery is very useful
for multiplying large numbers, so let’s

00:02:45.249 --> 00:02:47.540
move on to that topic next.

